A group structure can look simple on an organization chart and become complicated the moment finance, sales, inventory, and people need to work in the same system. The question, **can Odoo support multiple companies**, has a clear answer: yes. Odoo has native multi-company capabilities. But whether it works well for your business depends on how you define legal entities, shared operations, user access, and financial responsibility before configuration begins.

For an SME group, the goal is rarely to put every entity into one database at any cost. The goal is controlled visibility where it creates value, with enough separation to keep accounting, compliance, and accountability clean.

## Can Odoo Support Multiple Companies in One Database?

Yes. Odoo can run several companies in a single database. Each company can have its own legal identity, address, tax settings, fiscal positions, bank accounts, journals, chart of accounts, warehouses, and financial reports. Users can be assigned to one company or several, with rights that determine what they can see and do.

That structure suits many common situations: a Belgian parent company with a French sales entity, separate operating companies for different business lines, or a holding company that needs oversight without handling day-to-day transactions. It can also work where a group operates multiple brands but shares a back-office team.

The practical advantage is not simply fewer logins. A well-designed multi-company environment reduces duplicate master data, supports controlled reporting, and makes it easier for central teams to work across the group. Salespeople can quote from the correct entity. Finance can close the books per company. Management can compare performance without merging spreadsheets every month.

There is a limit, however. Multiple companies in one Odoo database should not become an excuse to blur legal boundaries. Separate entities remain separate entities. If each company has different accounting rules, approval processes, product strategies, or operating models, those differences need to be designed into the system.

## What Can Be Shared and What Should Stay Separate?

The answer is partly technical and mostly operational. Odoo lets you share selected records across companies or restrict them to a specific company. The right choice depends on who owns the data and whether using the same record creates clarity or confusion.

### Data that groups often share

Products are frequently shared, especially where companies sell the same catalog, source from the same suppliers, or use [central purchasing](/en/industries-wholesale-b2b-sales/). Contacts may also be shared, which prevents the same customer or vendor from being created repeatedly with slightly different names and addresses.

A shared product record does not mean every company must use the same selling price, revenue account, tax treatment, or replenishment route. Those settings can vary by company when needed. This is useful, but it requires disciplined configuration. Otherwise, a change intended for one entity can affect another.

Employees, project templates, website content, and CRM records can be shared in some group models. Whether they should be shared is another question. A common sales team may need cross-company customer visibility. Two autonomous companies competing in different markets may not.

### Data that usually needs company-level control

Accounting must be treated carefully. Each legal entity normally needs its own journals, tax configuration, bank accounts, payment methods, fiscal year settings, and statutory reporting. Even where a group uses a standardized chart of accounts, local requirements may call for exceptions.

Warehouses should be separated when stock is legally owned by different companies or physically managed by distinct entities. The same applies to inventory valuation. A product can be shared while stock ownership remains company-specific. That distinction matters when goods move between entities.

Customers and suppliers can also need separation in sensitive cases. An organization may share a group-wide contact directory while limiting commercial documents, pricing, and financial data to the company handling the relationship.

## Intercompany Transactions Need Real Processes

One of the strongest reasons to use Odoo multi-company is to manage transactions between entities. A central purchasing company may sell goods to local operating companies. A service company may invoice management fees to subsidiaries. One entity may [fulfill orders](/en/solution-transport-logistics/) on behalf of another.

Odoo can support intercompany workflows, including automated creation of corresponding sales and purchase documents between companies. Properly configured, this reduces rekeying and makes related transactions easier to trace.

Automation does not remove the need for policy. You still need to decide which entity owns inventory at every point, which transfer prices apply, who approves internal invoices, how VAT is handled, and when revenue and costs are recognized. These are finance and operational decisions first. The ERP should enforce the chosen process, not invent it.

For example, if a Belgian distribution company purchases stock and a French entity sells it, the group must define whether stock is transferred before the customer sale, sold on consignment, or invoiced through another arrangement. Each model has different implications for inventory, margin, tax, and reporting. A shortcut in configuration can create a reconciliation problem at year-end.

## User Access Is Where Multi-Company Projects Succeed or Fail

A multi-company setup is only as reliable as its access model. Owners and group finance leaders often want broad visibility. Local teams need enough access to do their jobs, but not unrestricted access to every entity’s data.

Odoo allows users to have access to one or multiple companies and to switch their active company context. Users can also be granted access to several companies while working in one at a time. This is valuable for central finance, shared customer service, and group management.

The risk is giving broad access because it feels easier during implementation. That decision can expose confidential salaries, customer margins, supplier terms, or financial records. It can also lead users to create documents in the wrong company, particularly when teams work quickly across several entities.

Start with roles, not individual preferences. Define what a local accountant, group controller, purchaser, warehouse manager, sales manager, and director must do. Then test realistic scenarios: creating a quote, receiving stock, approving a bill, issuing a credit note, and running reports. Permission problems are cheaper to fix before go-live than after a month-end close.

## One Database or Separate Odoo Databases?

A single multi-company database is often the right choice when entities share customers, suppliers, products, employees, processes, or central services. It offers a more consistent operating model and reduces the integration work needed to create group visibility.

Separate databases may be better when companies are highly independent, use very different processes, operate under materially different regulatory constraints, or need strict information barriers. This can also make sense after an acquisition, when the group needs reporting visibility quickly but is not ready to standardize operations.

There is a trade-off. Separate databases can protect autonomy, but they add duplication and make consolidated reporting harder. One database can improve control and efficiency, but it requires stronger governance and more careful implementation. Neither option is automatically cheaper over time.

A useful test is to ask where your teams currently lose time. If they repeatedly copy customer information, manually recharge costs, reconcile internal stock movements, or build group reports in Excel, a multi-company setup may produce a clear return. If the entities barely interact and need separate systems for legitimate reasons, forcing them together may create more friction than value.

## A Practical Setup Sequence for SME Groups

Before anyone creates a second company record, document the group model. Identify every legal entity, its countries of operation, accounting obligations, VAT registrations, currencies, warehouses, and bank accounts. Then map which teams, products, customers, suppliers, and services are genuinely shared.

Next, define the intercompany flows. Include stock transfers, internal sales, management charges, shared service costs, and any cross-company fulfillment. Finance should approve these rules alongside operations leadership. A [multi-company ERP project](/en/odoo-erp/) should never leave transfer pricing and invoice ownership to assumptions.

Then build the access matrix and reporting requirements. Decide which reports each role needs, whether management needs consolidated views, and which data must remain private. Configure a pilot using real transactions rather than sample data. Month-end scenarios, tax reporting, returns, and exceptions will reveal issues that a basic demonstration will not.

Finally, set ownership for ongoing changes. Adding a company, changing a shared product, creating a new warehouse, or altering account mappings can affect more than one entity. Someone must have the authority and knowledge to review those changes.

Odoo can give a growing group one operational backbone without erasing the identity of each company. The best starting point is a candid design workshop: map the legal reality, the daily work, and the reporting expectations before choosing what should be shared.
